capability address/value  an IPv4 or IPv6 address as a value, with a port where one belongs
  heroes  net.Address
  go      netip.Addr, netip.AddrPort, net.IP, net.IPAddr, net.TCPAddr, net.UDPAddr, net.Addr, netip.Addr.IsValid, netip.AddrPort.IsValid
  rust    IpAddr, IpAddr::V4, IpAddr::V6, Ipv4Addr, Ipv6Addr, SocketAddr, SocketAddr::V4, SocketAddr::V6, SocketAddrV4, SocketAddrV6
  python  ipaddress.IPv4Address, ipaddress.IPv6Address
  note    One record for the four shapes Go and Rust split: the bytes say the kind, `has_port` whether a port belongs. No zero value stands for "no address", so Go's `IsValid` has nothing to ask

capability address/parse  an address read from text, without a port
  heroes  net.parse_address
  go      netip.ParseAddr, netip.MustParseAddr, netip.Addr.UnmarshalText, net.ParseIP, net.IP.UnmarshalText
  rust    IpAddr::from_str, Ipv4Addr::from_str, Ipv6Addr::from_str, IpAddr::parse_ascii, Ipv4Addr::parse_ascii, Ipv6Addr::parse_ascii, AddrParseError
  python  ipaddress.ip_address, ipaddress.AddressValueError, socket.inet_pton, socket.inet_aton
  note    Go and Python agree on every edge asked (a number with a zero before it, `::` standing for no group, IPv4 only in the last two groups); a zone is non-empty and holds no `%` or `/` as Python reads it, nor `]` or a control byte or space, which Python takes and which would not read back after brackets; Go takes any zone, Rust's `IpAddr` none. `inet_aton`'s old forms (`127.1`, octal) are refused as Go and Python's `ipaddress` refuse them

capability address/parse-with-port  an address and a port read from text, IPv6 in brackets
  heroes  net.parse_address_port
  go      netip.ParseAddrPort, netip.MustParseAddrPort, netip.AddrPort.UnmarshalText
  rust    SocketAddr::from_str, SocketAddrV4::from_str, SocketAddrV6::from_str, SocketAddr::parse_ascii, SocketAddrV4::parse_ascii, SocketAddrV6::parse_ascii
  python  -
  note    Go's: zeros before the port read (`080` is 80), IPv4 in brackets and IPv6 without them refused

capability address/format  an address written as text that reads back to it
  heroes  net.format_address
  go      netip.Addr.String, netip.Addr.AppendTo, netip.Addr.AppendText, netip.Addr.MarshalText, netip.AddrPort.String, netip.AddrPort.AppendTo, netip.AddrPort.AppendText, netip.AddrPort.MarshalText, net.IP.String, net.IP.AppendText, net.IP.MarshalText, net.IPAddr.String, net.TCPAddr.String, net.UDPAddr.String
  rust    Display of IpAddr, Ipv4Addr, Ipv6Addr, SocketAddr, SocketAddrV4, SocketAddrV6 (to_string)
  python  str(IPv4Address), IPv4Address.compressed, IPv6Address.compressed, socket.inet_ntop, socket.inet_ntoa
  note    RFC 5952 as all three write it: lower case, the first longest run of two zero groups or more as `::`, a mapped address dotted; Go and Python wrote the same text for all 24 addresses both read

capability address/format-expanded  an address written with every digit
  heroes  net.format_address_expanded
  go      netip.Addr.StringExpanded
  rust    -
  python  IPv4Address.exploded, IPv6Address.exploded
  note    Go's for a mapped address, every group in hexadecimal; Python keeps its last 32 bits dotted

